WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:19.200
Leonardo da Vinci was a genius who invented
revolutionary ideas centuries before his the

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.970
rest of the world would catch up with him.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:27.410
He embodies the definition of a “Renaissance
Man”- one who studied and mastered a variety

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:28.580
of different subjects.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:33.110
He was a painter, engineer, architect, writer,
scientist, and inventor.

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:37.980
While you may know him for his most famous
works of art like The Mona Lisa or The Last

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.770
Supper, few people know the man behind the
paintings.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:51.430
Today on Biographics, we’re talking about
Leonardo da Vinci.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:01:00.400
Early Life
A baby boy named Leonardo was born on April

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.159
15th, 1452 in a Tuscan village called Vinci.

00:01:04.159 --> 00:01:09.850
The child was born out of wedlock to a wealthy
legal notary named Messer Piero Fruosino di

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:14.290
Antonio, and a beautiful peasant girl named
Caterina di Meo Lippi.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:18.340
His father took responsibility for the boy,
and raised him on the family estate with his

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:19.340
wife.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.230
But as a bastard child, young Leonardo was
not even allowed to be given his father’s

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:24.230
last name.

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:28.880
He was simply called “Leonardo da Vinci”
which means “Leonardo, of Vinci.”

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:33.760
Leonardo lived with his biological mother
until he was 5, and his father took over the

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.530
responsibility of raising him.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:39.620
Caterina went on to marry a man who was simply
referred to as “the fighter”.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:44.960
Leonardo’s father, Messer, married a 16-year
old noble girl named Albiera, and they had

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:46.890
legitimate children of their own.

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:50.760
Instead of going to school, the countryside
was young Leonardo’s classroom.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:55.210
Even at a young age, he was an inquisitive,
and truly fell in love with nature.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:02:00.700
He wished that men could fly like birds, and
swim through the ocean like fish.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:05.800
Together with his brothers, they played games
of war, and he imagined being triumphant in

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.849
battle, like the men in the stories told by
his father.

00:02:09.849 --> 00:02:12.100
However, this stage of innocence could not
last forever.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:17.480
As he grew up, Leonardo learned that he would
not be allowed to inherit the family estate.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.660
Leonardo had 9 brothers and 3 sisters.

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:23.510
So, even if he could inherit his father’s
estate, it’s not like there would be much

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.209
left to go around, anyway.

00:02:25.209 --> 00:02:30.189
He had to learn a trade and figure out how
to take care of himself.

00:02:30.189 --> 00:02:35.810
In medieval Florence, guilds were like trade
unions, and they were split up into multiple

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:40.510
categories of jobs that a man could choose
from, so long as he was born into the right

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:41.840
social class.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.780
The highest ranking guilds were called the
“Arti Maggiori”, and his father would

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.889
have been apart of the guild of powerful lawyers,
judges, and notaries.

00:02:49.889 --> 00:02:54.209
But as an illegitimate child, this was like
being in the lower class, and he was not even

00:02:54.209 --> 00:02:57.300
allowed to join the lesser guild categories
like “Arti Mediane” , which had the middle

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.799
class skills like blacksmiths and stone masons.

00:03:00.799 --> 00:03:03.379
His only option would be to join a guild in
the “Arti Minori”, which had lower-class

00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:05.959
jobs like bakers and millers.

00:03:05.959 --> 00:03:09.870
However, Leonardo’s father still wanted
his son to have a good life.

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:12.319
He could see that the boy had a natural talent
for art.

00:03:12.319 --> 00:03:17.030
So, his father introduced him to a man named
Andre del Verrocchio, who ran an art studio

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:18.469
in the city of Florence.

00:03:18.469 --> 00:03:22.609
After seeing the boy’s sketches, he agreed
to take him on as an apprentice.

00:03:22.609 --> 00:03:28.560
The Florence Guild of Artists

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.469
Andre del Verrocchio had several artists and
craftsmen working for him in his workshop.

00:03:33.469 --> 00:03:37.849
He would take on commissions from the wealthy
and powerful Medici family, and divide the

00:03:37.849 --> 00:03:40.389
work among his students and employees.

00:03:40.389 --> 00:03:43.809
Their studio worked on improving the beauty
of the city.

00:03:43.809 --> 00:03:48.609
They had to work with all mediums, whether
it was carving marble statues, casting metal,

00:03:48.609 --> 00:03:49.609
or painting portraits.

00:03:49.609 --> 00:03:55.040
Even architecture, bridges, and other forms
of civil engineering were all sketched by

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:56.519
the members of the guild.

00:03:56.519 --> 00:04:00.659
So it became necessary for Leonardo to learn
multiple disciplines.

00:04:00.659 --> 00:04:05.459
After a few years of training, Leonardo da
Vinci assisted Verrocchio in painting a commissioned

00:04:05.459 --> 00:04:08.909
piece for the Catholic Church called “The
Baptism of Christ”.

00:04:08.909 --> 00:04:12.599
This was a very large mural on the wall of
a church, so there was plenty of space for

00:04:12.599 --> 00:04:16.269
two artists to stand and work on the painting
at the same time.

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:21.060
Verrocchio instructed Leonardo to paint two
angels that had been included in his rough

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.060
sketch.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:24.500
When Da Vinci was done, Verrocchio was in
shock.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:28.389
He could not believe how gorgeous and life-like
the painting was.

00:04:28.389 --> 00:04:32.420
He knew that the student had already surpassed
the master.

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:36.840
By his mid-20’s, Leonardo Da Vinci became
a master artist in Verrocchio’s studio,

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:38.300
and he earned a reputation for himself in
Florence.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:43.010
Leonardo conducted experiments with his art,
like studying how light changed the shading

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:47.190
on an object, or the perfect time of day to
sketch someone’s face.

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:52.230
However, his talent was also a curse, because
it caused a lot of jealousy among the art

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:53.230
community.

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:57.910
At 24 years old, Da Vinci was accused of having
sex with a male prostitute, and he was arrested

00:04:57.910 --> 00:04:58.910
for sodomy.

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:02.720
At that time, the punishment for sodomy was
being burned to death.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:08.110
But when he went to trial, no evidence or
witnesses were presented, and they could not

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.070
prove that he did anything illegal.

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:12.020
So they let him go.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:16.490
However, even though he was found “not guilty”,
he wrote in his journals that this scandal

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:18.620
very seriously hurt his reputation.

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:22.520
Fewer clients wanted to work with him around
Florence, despite his obvious talent.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:27.830
We don’t know his actual sexual orientation,
except that he never got married.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:31.120
Some believe that he chose to be celibate
in order to focus more on his work.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:35.760
At the end of the day, whoever turned him
into the authorities wanted him dead.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:41.170
As awful as this sounds, medieval times were
truly cut-throat, and men of high standing

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:45.460
always had to watch their backs to make sure
they were not killed by their enemies.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:49.920
An assassin named Bernardo di Bandino Baroncelli
stabbed Giuliano de’ Medici in the streets

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:53.020
simply because they were both from rival banking
families.

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:56.720
One of Leonardo’s earliest known sketches
was his observation of Baroncelli’s body

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.170
in the gallows called “The hanged man”.

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:04.120
After this dispute between the Baroncellis
and the Medici families, Florence and Naples

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:05.260
were on the brink of war.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:10.180
The men in Da Vinci’s guild were forced
to stop working on art, and use their engineering

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:12.140
skills to defend the city, instead.

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:16.600
Since Da Vinci was a bastard, he could not
become a knight and serve in a royal court,

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:17.600
so he thought this was far more exciting than
painting Catholic murals.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:22.040
He started to design ladders and massive weapons
to help defend Florence, should they ever

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:23.040
need it.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:27.720
Perhaps he saw this as his way to be remembered
in the history books.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:31.310
During a three month time period, the city
was on edge, believing they were on the brink

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:32.310
of war.

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:36.150
When Lorenzo Medici returned after making
a successful peace treaty, the rest of the

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:40.220
city celebrated, and Leonardo was actually
disappointed.

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:44.020
After peace was returned to the city, the
artist’s guild could go back to making beautiful

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:48.800
things, but this was no longer challenging
or interesting to Leonardo Da Vinci.

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:52.970
When he was 29 years old, he was asked to
do his first commissioned mural at a church

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:54.110
all by himself.

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:59.000
The painting was called “Adoration of the
Magi”, and he took his concept above and

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:02.200
beyond what any other painter had done in
the past.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:06.590
It was an incredible piece, but Da Vinci didn’t
seem to see that, because after spending a

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:11.650
very long time working on the piece, he decided
that he could not finish the painting.

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:16.990
He was very restless at that time, and didn’t
have the patience to actually sit and complete

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:17.990
a lot of his projects.

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:22.830
He had so many brilliant ideas, he sometimes
had had trouble executing them.

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:28.010
Da Vinci was actually sued more than once
for getting paid by a patron, and then failing

00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:29.880
to finish his work, as promised.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:35.370
With this, coupled with the accusation of
sodomy, his talent was not enough to make

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:37.190
up for his bad reputation.

00:07:37.190 --> 00:08:13.590
He thought that maybe, it was time to start
over somewhere new.

00:08:13.590 --> 00:09:03.300
A New Life in Milan

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:06.899
Even though he was more talented than many
of the other painters in Florence, Leonardo

00:09:06.899 --> 00:09:10.589
Da Vinci still went through a midlife crisis
when he was 30 years old.

00:09:10.589 --> 00:09:15.220
He realized that the lifestyle of a professional
artist did not suit him, and he craved the

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:18.370
adventure of having a career in weapon designs.

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:23.370
He had an entire book filled with incredible
inventions that would not be made until years

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:30.269
later, like a tank, a giant crossbow, underwater
scuba gear, and even a robotic soldier.

00:09:30.269 --> 00:09:35.740
He was truly hundreds of years ahead of his
time, but without a war, these weapons would

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:37.269
never actually come to life.

00:09:37.269 --> 00:09:41.829
Leonardo da Vinci planned to move to Milan,
because it is the most northern Italian state,

00:09:41.829 --> 00:09:47.320
but he wanted to secure employment before
he made the journey.

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:53.290
Since the city is much closer to France, they
were far more interested in gathering weapons

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:54.829
to defend the country.

00:09:54.829 --> 00:09:59.790
He sent a letter to the Duke of Milan, claiming
that he was a weapon’s design expert, and

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:01.839
included a few pages from his sketchbook.

00:10:01.839 --> 00:10:05.800
At the very end of the letter, he included
a single line mentioning that he was also

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:06.800
a painter.

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:12.110
Ludovico Sforza was the temporary Duke of
Milan, holding the position of power until

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:13.790
his nephew came of age.

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:17.240
The Sforza family had not been in power for
very long, especially compared to the likes

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:18.639
of the Medicis.

00:10:18.639 --> 00:10:23.389
Sforza invited Leonardo da Vinci to visit,
and he was overjoyed, hoping that he would

00:10:23.389 --> 00:10:25.459
finally be able to make a career change.

00:10:25.459 --> 00:10:30.529
But, once he got there, he was gravely disappointed
to find out that the Duke wanted him to paint

00:10:30.529 --> 00:10:32.480
a portrait of his beautiful mistress.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:35.459
Today, that portrait is called “Lady with
an Ermine”.

00:10:35.459 --> 00:10:40.680
Even though he came to Milan to escape being
a painter, he still blew people away with

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:41.870
his talent.

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:46.540
Sforza was so impressed, he commissioned a
statue of a 24-foot tall horse.

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:49.899
Da Vinci told him that it would take at least
3 years to complete.

00:10:49.899 --> 00:10:54.040
In reality, Da Vinci was stalling for time,
because without the help of his master’s

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:59.360
studio, he had no idea how he was going to
pull off a 24-foot tall metal cast horse.

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:00.550
Remember- this was back in the 1400’s, so
there was no benefit of modern machinery to

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:01.550
help him.

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:04.129
The Sforzas were still establishing themselves,
and the Duke of Milan believed that paying

00:11:04.129 --> 00:11:08.279
for things like metal statues would somehow
prove their permanence in high society.

00:11:08.279 --> 00:11:13.630
At first, Da Vinci created the horse out of
clay, and it was unveiled at a party, where

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:17.980
Da Vinci was officially accepted into the
Sforza royal court.

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:22.580
Unfortunately, just like a lot of his projects,
DaVinci was a bit in over is head, and he

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:23.809
was running out of time.

00:11:23.809 --> 00:11:28.519
By a stroke of luck, Sforza decided to go
to war, and he needed the metal resources

00:11:28.519 --> 00:11:30.009
for his army’s weapons.

00:11:30.009 --> 00:11:35.519
Yet again, Da Vinci thought this might be
his time to shine with his weapon designs,

00:11:35.519 --> 00:11:39.089
but there weren’t enough resources to pay
for their creation.

00:11:39.089 --> 00:11:42.920
The Duke of Milan game him a new assignment
of decorating a church called Santa Maria

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:47.689
delle Grazie, which is where he painted one
of his most famous works, “The Last Supper.”

00:11:47.689 --> 00:11:51.920
At the time, the priests were actually very
annoyed with him, because it took him over

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:55.579
3 years to complete the painting, which is
a mural on the wall of the church.

00:11:55.579 --> 00:12:01.369
For 17 years, DaVinci enjoyed a life of financial
security and respect in Sforza’s royal court.

00:12:01.369 --> 00:12:05.880
During the time that he lived in Milan, Da
Vinci had a secret project that he never dared

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:06.880
to tell anyone, and it is likely a good reason
why the rest of his work was taking so long.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:11.850
He was dissecting human cadavers and sketching
their bodies, muscles, and organs.

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:13.970
He also made wax casts of the organs.

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:18.470
Yes, this sounds incredibly creepy, and he
realized this, which is why he kept his sketchbook

00:12:18.470 --> 00:12:21.819
a secret, and it only showed up in the 1600’s.

00:12:21.819 --> 00:12:26.040
At that time, autopsies and any other form
of disturbing the dead was frowned upon by

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:27.180
the Catholic Church.

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:30.679
He already knew what it was like to have a
soiled reputation back in Florence, so he

00:12:30.679 --> 00:12:35.300
was far more careful this time while leading
a double life.

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:38.860
Even though his sketchbook was found over
100 years later, his studies of the human

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:43.579
body were revolutionary, and became the foundation
of modern anatomy.

00:12:43.579 --> 00:12:48.999
Leonardo da Vinci would write in notebooks
about his scientific experiments, inventions,

00:12:48.999 --> 00:12:50.610
and artistic ideas.

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:54.120
There are over 15,000 pages of his notes that
survived.

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:58.240
He even wrote some of those notes backwards,
so you can only read them when looking in

00:12:58.240 --> 00:12:59.240
a mirror.

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:02.120
Some people believe he did this because he
was worried that his rivals would steal his

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:05.699
ideas.

00:13:05.699 --> 00:13:11.559
Homecoming
When Leonardo Da Vinci was 50 years old, the

00:13:11.559 --> 00:13:15.350
Sforza family lost their position of power,
and the French invaded Milan.

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:20.319
When they saw the giant clay horse statue
standing on the estate, they used it for target

00:13:20.319 --> 00:13:21.319
practice.

00:13:21.319 --> 00:13:25.259
Leonardo da Vinci had to leave Milan with
the bare necessities, like his journals, and

00:13:25.259 --> 00:13:27.360
left all of his works of art behind.

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:32.269
He decided to travel from place to place,
once again trying to pitch his ideas for military

00:13:32.269 --> 00:13:33.990
weaponry around Italy.

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:38.420
He showed The Duke of Venice his ideas, which
included a snorkeling suit for breathing underwater

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:40.029
that actually worked.

00:13:40.029 --> 00:13:44.170
He had ideas on how humans could fly, or walk
on the bottom of the ocean.

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:49.529
Keep in mind, this was at a time when people
didn’t even have clocks or watches to tell

00:13:49.529 --> 00:13:50.529
time.

00:13:50.529 --> 00:13:54.079
While all of his ideas were amazing, they
were far too ahead of his time, and the Duke

00:13:54.079 --> 00:13:57.290
of Venice didn’t find a need for any of
those inventions.

00:13:57.290 --> 00:14:02.589
Da Vinci was forced to reluctantly move back
to Florence, because he feared that everyone

00:14:02.589 --> 00:14:04.829
would still remember his bad reputation.

00:14:04.829 --> 00:14:10.199
But it was actually far worse than that, because
by that time, people had all but forgotten

00:14:10.199 --> 00:14:14.730
the name Leonardo Da Vinci, and there was
a new genius in town: Michelangelo di Lodovico

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:15.730
Buonarroti Simoni.

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:16.730
But you know as simply “Michelangelo”.

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:17.730
He was just 26 at that time.

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:22.180
Since the art world in Florence was so competitive,
Leonardo hated Michelangelo almost immediately,

00:14:22.180 --> 00:14:23.180
because this meant that he would struggle
finding work.

00:14:23.180 --> 00:14:27.499
Da Vinci was clearly jealous, and had to find
reasons to dislike Michelangelo.

00:14:27.499 --> 00:14:32.170
He would write angrily in his journal about
how he thought the young man was dirty and

00:14:32.170 --> 00:14:33.490
undignified.

00:14:33.490 --> 00:14:37.310
Despite the long beard you see in most depictions
of Da Vinci, he actually spent all of his

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:42.899
younger years making sure he was always clean-shaven,
and wore the finest clothes.

00:14:42.899 --> 00:14:47.019
He even had professional musicians playing
music for his clients while they sat for their

00:14:47.019 --> 00:14:51.370
portraits, and the whole process felt like
going to a luxury spa.

00:14:51.370 --> 00:14:54.750
Michelangelo, on the other hand, had the polar
opposite approach to his art.

00:14:54.750 --> 00:14:58.910
He was so focused on his work, that he would
forget to shave for a few days, and let his

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:00.149
beard grow.

00:15:00.149 --> 00:15:04.869
He let chips of stone and dust cover his home
after working on a statue, and it was far

00:15:04.869 --> 00:15:08.989
more like a workshop than a comfortable place
to clients to stay.

00:15:08.989 --> 00:15:14.040
The two artists deeply disliked each other,
and even got into a fight out in public.

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:18.850
Secretly, Da Vinci would go home to write
about how depressed he was to be getting older,

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:23.610
and the fear and anxiety rules his thoughts.

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:28.910
Be Careful What You Wish For

00:15:28.910 --> 00:15:32.449
Since Da Vinci could no longer secure his
place in the Florence art scene, he decided

00:15:32.449 --> 00:15:36.050
to try his luck at weapon design one last
time.

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:39.519
Pope Alexander had a bastard son named Cesare
Borgia.

00:15:39.519 --> 00:15:42.850
Some people say that he was a great leader,
while others say that Borgia was a bloodthirsty

00:15:42.850 --> 00:15:45.220
ruler who killed anyone who got in his way.

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:49.329
Machiavelli's famous book called The Prince
was even based on Borgia as an example of

00:15:49.329 --> 00:15:52.009
what a ruler must be in order to stay in power.

00:15:52.009 --> 00:15:56.720
Da Vinci sent his sketches to Borgia, and
finally, someone said yes to the weapons.

00:15:56.720 --> 00:16:01.089
He become Chief General Engineer for Cesare
Borgia, and supervised the construction of

00:16:01.089 --> 00:16:05.579
towers, weapons, and bird’s-eye-view maps
of the cities he planned on invading, which

00:16:05.579 --> 00:16:07.230
was revolutionary for the time.

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:12.209
Da Vinci got everything he wanted, but it
was at a price he never expected.

00:16:12.209 --> 00:16:15.649
The only reason why Borgia was willing to
pay for the creation of all of these weapons

00:16:15.649 --> 00:16:19.319
was because he wanted to kill as many enemies
as possible.

00:16:19.319 --> 00:16:24.309
Until this point, the idea of war had been
simply a concept to Leonardo Da Vinci, and

00:16:24.309 --> 00:16:27.579
stories of knights going to battle seemed
like good fun.

00:16:27.579 --> 00:16:32.550
For the first time in his life, Da Vinci actually
witnessed what a bloody battle looked like,

00:16:32.550 --> 00:16:34.999
and it was too gruesome for him to handle.

00:16:34.999 --> 00:16:40.920
He wrote, “Truly, man is more savage than
beast, for our brutality exceeds theirs.

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:43.589
We live by the death of others.”

00:16:43.589 --> 00:16:47.350
The straw that broke the camel’s back was
when Borgia ordered the death of one of Da

00:16:47.350 --> 00:16:48.490
Vinci’s close friends.

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:53.389
He quietly left Borgia in the dead of night
and moved back to Florence, where he returned

00:16:53.389 --> 00:16:57.029
to a peaceful life of painting portraits.

00:16:57.029 --> 00:17:04.130
Mona Lisa’s Smile
After selling those weapons, Leonardo da Vinci

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:08.799
had saved a nice nest egg for retirement,
and It was during this time that he painted

00:17:08.799 --> 00:17:11.910
“The Mona Lisa” No one knows for sure
who she actually is.

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:13.910
There are several theories of who she is,
or what she might represent, but none have

00:17:13.910 --> 00:17:15.280
been confirmed.

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:19.500
Unlike all of those portraits that were commissioned
and given away, he carried around the portrait

00:17:19.500 --> 00:17:22.190
of Mona Lisa with him for the rest of his
life.

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:26.290
For once, this was a piece of art that was
done just for himself, but he was constantly

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:30.120
changing it, because he never felt he got
it exactly right.

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:34.670
During his retirement, he started to build
massive wings for human beings, in hopes that

00:17:34.670 --> 00:17:36.790
it would help people fly.

00:17:36.790 --> 00:17:38.290
“There will be wings for man.

00:17:38.290 --> 00:17:43.650
If it is not accomplished by me, it will be
accomplished some day, by some other.”

00:17:43.650 --> 00:17:47.540
There is a commonly told story that during
a flight attempt with these wings, someone

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:49.340
fell and broke their leg.

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:53.870
After this failed attempt, he designed a motor
that would flap the wings, but it never worked

00:17:53.870 --> 00:17:54.870
properly.

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:58.650
However, he did eventually design the first
wing that looks very much like what we use

00:17:58.650 --> 00:17:59.820
in modern-day airplanes.

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:04.860
Aside from the patrons that he served, Leonardo
da Vinci was not yet famous around the world.

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:08.330
He intended to publish books, but he never
actually went through with it.

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:12.950
As we mentioned earlier, he wrote and sketched
over 15,000 pages on a variety of subjects,

00:18:12.950 --> 00:18:14.500
but he was a perfectionist.

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:20.320
So he continued to add on more and more information,
because he never felt that they were complete.

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:23.810
Among these notes, there are multiple sketches
of an old man with a beard.

00:18:23.810 --> 00:18:27.780
People assume that this was his self-portrait,
but no one knows for sure.

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:32.780
However, this image is used to portray him
in popular culture, and it’s how he is remembered

00:18:32.780 --> 00:18:34.230
today.

00:18:34.230 --> 00:18:38.730
King Francis I in France had been collecting
Da Vinci’s pieces for years, and he wrote

00:18:38.730 --> 00:18:42.270
a letter to invite him to live in his chateau.

00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:46.430
He agreed to move to France, and when he was
65 years old, and he had a stroke, which paralyzed

00:18:46.430 --> 00:18:47.430
his arm.

00:18:47.430 --> 00:18:52.020
The king’s castle became his comfortable
retirement, and he was given the best medical

00:18:52.020 --> 00:18:53.560
care money could buy.

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:58.750
When he died in the year 1519, he left the
Mona Lisa and all of his sketchbooks and journals

00:18:58.750 --> 00:19:00.120
to his loyal apprentice.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:03.970
Born a bastard, Leonardo Da Vinci died like
a king.

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:08.910
His life lives on forever in his work, and
he continued to serve as an inspiration to

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:22.540
people around the world for centuries

00:19:22.540 --> 00:19:26.020
to come.

